In 1827 Georg Ohm measured how much current flowed through wires of different lengths and thicknesses when connected to a source. He found a simple, fixed relationship: for a given conductor, **current is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance**. Double the voltage and the current doubles; double the resistance and the current halves.

That relationship, **V = I × R**, is Ohm's law. You will use it to:

- predict how much current a load will draw (so you can size breakers, conductors and overload devices);
- find the resistance of a load from its nameplate ratings;
- calculate voltage drop on long runs;
- make sense of meter readings while troubleshooting ("I have 120 V here but only 2 A: the load is acting like 60 Ω instead of the 12 Ω this heater should measure, so one of its parallel elements has probably burned open").

Ohm's law holds exactly for **resistive** loads (heaters, incandescent lamps once warm, long conductors) on DC and on AC. Motors, transformers and electronic drives add another kind of opposition called reactance; for those, a later lesson introduces impedance (Z), which slides into the same formula as V = I × Z.
